{{#> layout}}
{{> flow-header}}

<section class="example-section" data-server="http://127.0.0.1:8765" data-home="{{homeHref}}">
  {{> datadir-field}}

  <section class="part">
  <h2>Part 1: Get the public descriptors</h2>
  <p>This device needs the public descriptors of your wallet to create transactions. Type them in from the offline device, or load them from a wallet that already exists in Bitcoin Core on this device. Then choose the address type you will create transactions with.</p>

  <form id="source-form" class="phonetic-form" autocomplete="off" novalidate>
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend>Get the public descriptors by</legend>
      <label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="source" value="received" checked> Typing the Super Base 58 lines from the offline device</label>
      <label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="source" value="wallet"> Loading a wallet that already exists in Bitcoin Core on this device</label>
    </fieldset>
  </form>

  <div id="source-received">
    <p>Type the Super Base 58 (SB58) lines shown in Part 4 of the offline device guide, one line per line, then the final checksum on the last line. This page recovers the public descriptors from them. Input stays on this page and is not sent or saved.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Type each word and press <kbd>Space</kbd>. The word completes from its first letters when only one word starts with them, so <code>x</code> becomes <code>x-ray</code> and <code>sev</code> becomes <code>seven</code>.</li>
      <li>A few letters are shared by several words (<code>t</code> is tango, two or three), so type enough letters to tell them apart. The page tells you when it cannot complete a word.</li>
      <li>Each line is 4 words, the casing string such as <code>01101</code>, and the checksum word. Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> after the checksum word to start the next line.</li>
      <li>The last line is the 5-digit final checksum. Errors name the line to fix.</li>
    </ul>

    <div id="descriptor-receiver">
      <div class="sb58-editor">
        <div class="sb58-gutter" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <textarea class="sb58-input" rows="14" wrap="off" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off" aria-label="SB58 lines" aria-describedby="sb58-status sb58-hint sb58-errors"></textarea>
      </div>
      <p id="sb58-hint" class="sb58-hint" role="alert"></p>
      <p id="sb58-status" class="sb58-status" role="status"></p>
      <ul id="sb58-errors" class="sb58-errors" role="alert"></ul>

      <div class="sb58-result" hidden>
        <h3>Public descriptors</h3>
        <pre class="sb58-output" tabindex="0"></pre>
        <button type="button" class="sb58-copy">Copy public descriptors</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="source-wallet" hidden>
    <p>Enter the name of the wallet. The local server asks Bitcoin Core for its public descriptors with <code>listdescriptors</code>, loading the wallet first if it is not loaded, and never asks for private keys. Bitcoin Core must be running on this device. In a terminal, from the project folder, create a Python environment and install the dependencies (this needs internet access):</p>
    {{#> code-block}}python3 -m venv python/venv
source python/venv/bin/activate
pip3 install -r python/requirements.txt{{/code-block}}
    <p>Then start the local server and leave it running (run <code>source python/venv/bin/activate</code> first in a new terminal). This page must also be served on port 8000, for example with <code>python3 -m http.server 8000</code> from the project folder and opened at <code>http://localhost:8000/build/</code>, or the server will refuse it:</p>
    {{#> code-block}}python3 python/server.py{{/code-block}}
    <p id="server-status" class="server-status" role="status">Checking for the local server…</p>

    <form id="wallet-form" class="phonetic-form" autocomplete="off" novalidate>
      <div class="phonetic-field">
        <label for="wallet-name">Wallet name</label>
        <input id="wallet-name" value="my-wallet" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off">
      </div>
    </form>

    <div class="notebook-cell" data-script="list-public-descriptors">
      <div class="notebook-toolbar">
        <button type="button" class="run-button" data-action="run">&#9654; Load descriptors</button>
        <button type="button" data-action="copy-code">Copy code</button>
        <span class="run-status" role="status"></span>
      </div>
      {{#> code-block caption="Python" language="python"}}{{scripts.listPublicDescriptors}}{{/code-block}}
      <div class="notebook-output" hidden aria-live="polite">
        <p class="notebook-output-label">Output</p>
        <pre class="result" tabindex="0"></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>To run it yourself, save the code as <code>list-public-descriptors.py</code> and run it with the environment above active:</p>
    {{#> code-block}}python3 list-public-descriptors.py my-wallet{{/code-block}}
  </div>

  <form id="type-form" class="phonetic-form" autocomplete="off" novalidate>
    <fieldset class="field-group" id="script-type-group" disabled>
      <legend>Address type (choose one)</legend>
      <label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="script-type" value="legacy"> Legacy (P2PKH)</label>
      <label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="script-type" value="nested-segwit"> Nested SegWit (P2SH-P2WPKH)</label>
      <label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="script-type" value="native-segwit"> Native SegWit (P2WPKH)</label>
      <label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="script-type" value="taproot"> Taproot (P2TR)</label>
    </fieldset>
  </form>
  <p id="type-status" class="source-status" role="status">Load the public descriptors to choose an address type.</p>
  <pre id="selected-descriptors" class="selected-descriptors" tabindex="0" hidden></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="part">
  <h2>Part 2: Import the public descriptors into Bitcoin Core</h2>
  <p><strong>Skip this part if you loaded the descriptors from a wallet in Part 1.</strong> That wallet already has them.</p>
  <p>This creates a new, empty watch-only wallet in Bitcoin Core and imports every public descriptor typed in Part 1, for all address types. To import into a watch-only wallet that already exists instead, uncheck "Create an empty wallet"; the wallet is loaded first if it is not loaded. A watch-only wallet has no private keys: it shows your balance and creates transactions, but only the offline device can sign them. Bitcoin Core must be running on this device. If you started it with a custom <code>-datadir</code>, enter it in the data directory field at the top of this page.</p>
  <p>The page runs the script through the local server. If it is not running yet, set up the Python environment and start it as described in Part 1 under "Loading a wallet", or in a terminal from the project folder:</p>
  {{#> code-block}}python3 -m venv python/venv
source python/venv/bin/activate
pip3 install -r python/requirements.txt
python3 python/server.py{{/code-block}}
  <p class="server-status" role="status">Checking for the local server…</p>
  <p>Bitcoin Core only finds the transactions made after the date you give below. For a new wallet that has never received bitcoin, leave it empty: the import is instant. If the wallet has received bitcoin before, enter the date it was created, or an earlier one. Bitcoin Core then scans the blockchain from that date, which can take hours, and the page waits until it is done. A pruned node cannot scan further back than the blocks it kept.</p>

  <form id="import-form" class="phonetic-form" autocomplete="off" novalidate>
    <div class="phonetic-field">
      <label for="import-wallet-name">Wallet name</label>
      <input id="import-wallet-name" value="my-wallet" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off">
      <label class="choice"><input type="checkbox" id="create-wallet" checked> Create an empty wallet with this name</label>
    </div>
    <div class="phonetic-field">
      <label for="rescan-since">Look for transactions since (optional)</label>
      <input id="rescan-since" type="date">
    </div>
  </form>

  <div class="notebook-cell" data-script="import-public-descriptors">
    <div class="notebook-toolbar">
      <button type="button" class="run-button" data-action="run">&#9654; Import descriptors</button>
      <button type="button" data-action="copy-code">Copy code</button>
      <span class="run-status" role="status"></span>
    </div>
    {{#> code-block caption="Python" language="python"}}{{scripts.importPublicDescriptors}}{{/code-block}}
    <div class="notebook-output" hidden aria-live="polite">
      <p class="notebook-output-label">Output</p>
      <pre class="result" tabindex="0"></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>To run it yourself, save the code as <code>import-public-descriptors.py</code> and run it with the environment above active. It reads the public descriptors from standard input, one per line. Add <code>--existing</code> to import into a wallet that already exists:</p>
  {{#> code-block}}python3 import-public-descriptors.py --wallet my-wallet --rescan-since 2024-01-31{{/code-block}}
  </section>

  <section class="part">
  <h2>Part 3: Create the transaction and transfer it to the offline device</h2>
  <p>This asks Bitcoin Core to create an unsigned transaction, a PSBT, from the watch-only wallet with <code>walletcreatefundedpsbt</code>. Bitcoin Core chooses the coins to spend and sends the change back to the wallet, to the address type chosen in Part 1. Nothing is signed or sent: only the offline device can sign it. The PSBT is then written as Super Base 58 lines to type on the offline device (see <a href="../intro/index.html">Super Base 58</a>).</p>
  <p>Check the destination address carefully, character by character. Bitcoin cannot be recovered once the signed transaction is sent.</p>

  <form id="psbt-form" class="phonetic-form" autocomplete="off" novalidate>
    <div class="phonetic-field">
      <label for="psbt-wallet-name">Wallet name</label>
      <input id="psbt-wallet-name" value="my-wallet" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off">
    </div>
    <div class="phonetic-field">
      <label for="destination">Destination address</label>
      <input id="destination" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off">
    </div>
    <div class="phonetic-field">
      <label for="amount">Amount</label>
      <input id="amount" inputmode="decimal" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off">
    </div>
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend>Amount in</legend>
      <label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="unit" value="btc" checked> BTC</label>
      <label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="unit" value="sats"> sats</label>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend>Fee</legend>
      <label class="choice"><input type="checkbox" id="subtract-fee"> Subtract the fee from the amount (the address receives the amount minus the fee, for example to send everything)</label>
      <div class="phonetic-field">
        <label for="fee-rate">Fee rate in sat/vB (optional, Bitcoin Core estimates one when empty)</label>
        <input id="fee-rate" inputmode="decimal" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off">
      </div>
    </fieldset>
  </form>

  <div class="notebook-cell" data-script="create-psbt">
    <div class="notebook-toolbar">
      <button type="button" class="run-button" data-action="run">&#9654; Create transaction</button>
      <button type="button" data-action="copy-code">Copy code</button>
      <span class="run-status" role="status"></span>
    </div>
    {{#> code-block caption="Python" language="python"}}{{scripts.createPsbt}}{{/code-block}}
    <div class="notebook-output" hidden aria-live="polite">
      <p class="notebook-output-label">Output</p>
      <pre class="result" tabindex="0"></pre>
      <div class="sb58-viewer" tabindex="0" hidden aria-label="Super Base 58 lines to type on the offline device">
        <div class="sb58-header">
          <p class="sb58-progress" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <button type="button" data-action="copy-lines">Copy lines</button>
          <span class="sb58-copy-status" role="status"></span>
        </div>
        <ol class="sb58-lines"></ol>
        <p class="sb58-help">Click here, then press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to move to the next line, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> to go back. Type each line on the offline device, then the final checksum last.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>To run it yourself, save the code as <code>create-psbt.py</code> and run it with the environment above active. It prints the PSBT in base64 and as SB58 lines:</p>
  {{#> code-block}}python3 create-psbt.py --wallet my-wallet --address bc1q... --amount 0.001 --unit btc --subtract-fee{{/code-block}}
  </section>

  <noscript>This page needs JavaScript enabled.</noscript>
</section>
<script src="{{assetsHref}}/notebook.js" defer></script>
<script src="{{assetsHref}}/sb58-viewer.js" defer></script>
<script src="{{assetsHref}}/sb58-receive.js" defer></script>
<script src="{{assetsHref}}/online-signing.js" defer></script>
{{> flow-navigation}}
{{/layout}}
